\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Minimizing extensions for the rational pairing}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Return to the rational pairing, operator and retained line of
\ref{fdip-00K4}, with the same chosen coordinate basis.
The component maps of \ref{fdip-00K7} are
##{\begin{aligned}
a&=2,& b(y,z)&=\tfrac32y,\\
d(x)&=(x,0),& c(y,z)&=(y,0).
\end{aligned}}
Thus #{N=\mathbb Qe_3}, #{W=\mathbb Qe_2}, and the coordinate
identifications #{X\simeq\mathbb Q}, #{W\simeq\mathbb Q} give
##{b_W=\tfrac32,\qquad d_W=1,\qquad c_W=1.}
The restricted pairings have coefficients #{2} and #{3}.
Their adjoint equation is #{2x(\tfrac32y)=3(1x)y}, so these
cross maps are adjoints despite their different entries.}

\p{The retained map and quadratic minimum are
##{S=2-\tfrac32\cdot1^{-1}\cdot1=\tfrac12,\qquad
   h_X(x,Sx)=x^2.}
The completed square is #{q_A(x,y,z)=x^2+3(y+x)^2}.
For each #{x}, every minimizer has #{y=-x}, with arbitrary
#{z\in\mathbb Q}; the full coset is #{-xe_2+\mathbb Qe_3}.
The embedded [shorted operator](fdip-00K6) is
##{T_0(x,y,z)=(\tfrac12x,0,0),\qquad
   q_{A-T_0}(x,y,z)=3(x+y)^2.}
Its greatestness among the specified admissible operators follows
from \ref{fdip-00K8}. The coefficient of the retained quadratic
form is #{1}, although the operator entry is #{1/2}.}

\p{Extend these same formulas from #{\mathbb Q} to #{\mathbb R}.
The full eliminated space is now #{U=\mathbb Re_2\oplus\mathbb Re_3},
with induced volume #{\sqrt{15}\,\extd y\,\extd z}.
For fixed #{x}, the weight
#{\exp(-x^2/2)\exp(-3(y+x)^2/2)} is independent of #{z}.
Its integral over any fixed compact #{y}-interval of positive length
is positive and finite. Integrating also over #{-R\leq z\leq R}
therefore grows as a positive constant times #{2R}.
The full #{U}-integral is infinite, both for coordinate volume and
for induced volume, even though #{S>0} on #{X}.}
\p{Integration over #{W=\mathbb Re_2} instead uses the induced
volume #{\sqrt3\,\extd y}. By the one-dimensional Gaussian integral,
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int_W \ue^{-q_A(i_Xx+i_Ujw)/2}\,\extd\lambda_W(w)\\
&\qquad=\sqrt{2\pi}\,\ue^{-x^2/2}.
\end{aligned}}
Coordinate volume #{\extd y} gives #{\sqrt{\frac{2\pi}{3}}\,\ue^{-x^2/2}}.
These are the changed-space and volume distinctions of
\ref{fdip-0039}; the algebraic calculation over #{\mathbb Q}
has introduced no measure there.}

\p{For a separate strictness diagnostic, choose standard coordinates
on #{\mathbb Q^2}, the counting pairing and the nonnegative operator
##{[A]=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
   q_A(x,y)=(x+y)^2.}
Eliminating the second coordinate gives #{c=1}, #{S=0} and the
unique minimizer #{y=-x}. An invertible eliminated compression
therefore need not give a strictly positive retained map.}
